---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';

const title = `Link policy — how ${SITE.name} decides what is dofollow`;
const description = `The exact rules behind every outbound link on ${SITE.name}: what qualifies for dofollow, what gets nofollowed, how links expire, and how to ask for a removal.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Link policy' }]} />
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Link policy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This page exists so that the rules are not a rumour. If anything here changes, the change lands on this page first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What a link here means</h2>
    <p>
      An outbound link on {SITE.name} is a statement that we opened the product, understood it well enough to describe it, and
      think it belongs in a list people trust. Nothing else is implied. There is no sponsorship tier, no "priority review"
      payment, and no affiliate arrangement with any listed product, so no link here should be read as a purchase.
    </p>

    <h2>When a link is dofollow</h2>
    <p>A link carries no value-affecting rel attribute only while all of the following hold:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the entry passed review and is marked approved;</li>
      <li>the target page returns a successful response on our last check;</li>
      <li>that check is more recent than {SITE.staleAfterDays} days;</li>
      <li>the entry carries at least {SITE.minDescriptionChars} characters of description we wrote ourselves.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If any of those fails, the link is rendered with <code>rel="nofollow ugc"</code> and the entry is dropped from the
      sitemap until it is fixed. The status is visible on every entry page, so nobody has to guess.
    </p>

    <h2>What is never accepted</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pages that require a login, a waitlist or a sales call to evaluate.</li>
      <li>Redirect chains, cloaking, or a submitted URL that is not the destination.</li>
      <li>Anything whose purpose is to hand out links: marketplaces of links, "get backlinks" services, guest-post farms.</li>
      <li>Gambling, crypto giveaways, adult content, medical or financial claims with no source.</li>
      <li>Domain squatting: one wish per product, and we consolidate clones.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Volume cap</h2>
    <p>
      No more than {SITE.dailyLaunchCap} entries go live per day and no more than {SITE.perRoundCap} per review round. This is
      a quality limit before it is an SEO one: a page that grows a hundred links a week stops being an index and starts being a
      link farm, which hurts the founders who listed in good faith.
    </p>

    <h2>Removals and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Want your entry taken down, retagged, or the wording changed? Email <a href={'mailto:' + SITE.email}>{SITE.email}</a> from the
      domain you submitted. Removals happen in the next review round, and we do not require a reason.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      Last reviewed {new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)}. The current version is deliberately short; when rules get long
      enough to need a lawyer, they are usually being written to justify a business model.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
